  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186083201,
      "id": "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00",
      "title": "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "8c489fd7-83de-59e0-8a3c-63bf109caf66",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}t\\,\\text{d}t$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{11}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{u-1}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{a-1}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}t\\,\\text{d}t & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(0-{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{a}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{11}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{11}^{a}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[4\\sqrt{x-2}]}_{11}^{a} \\\\  & =4{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt{a-2}-\\sqrt{11-2}) \\\\  & =4{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt{a-2}-3) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\nIn conclusion, only integrals II and III are divergent.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
